WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.608 --> 00:00:01.730
(applause)

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:02.563
- I'm sorry.

00:00:02.563 --> 00:00:03.980
I probably smell like static cling,

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:04.980
it's not my perfume.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.210
It's sticking to me.

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:08.670
So I like drenched myself.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:11.656
If you smell it, that's
not my Od Kelly Clarkson.

00:00:11.656 --> 00:00:12.520
(chuckling) Yeah, it's static cling.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:13.990
So how are you?

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:14.823
- I'm good.

00:00:14.823 --> 00:00:15.860
I'm excited to be here.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:17.010
- Yeah, I'm excited too.

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:18.290
This is such a cool project.

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:19.123
Before we get to that.

00:00:19.123 --> 00:00:20.390
How is Denmark?

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:21.940
I hear it's like the happiest place ever.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:23.310
Why?

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:24.143
- That's a good question,

00:00:24.143 --> 00:00:25.670
we keep asking ourselves that.

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:27.960
I think it's a mix of things.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.070
It's a great country.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.030
It's a small country.

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:33.088
There's only like 5 million of us.

00:00:33.088 --> 00:00:38.088
You can't really get
too carried away there

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.510
'cause everybody knows you
which I think is a good thing.

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:43.020
There's a high level of equality

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.460
and then there's these crazy things

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:46.920
like free healthcare
and free education and-

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.695
- See, it works, it works.

00:00:48.695 --> 00:00:49.528
(audience clapping)

00:00:49.528 --> 00:00:50.570
- It works for us.

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:51.403
I'm not-

00:00:51.403 --> 00:00:52.930
- I hear y'all know how
to vacation well too

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:53.903
you take time.

00:00:53.903 --> 00:00:54.736
- That's true, yeah.

00:00:54.736 --> 00:00:55.680
We have the whole balance

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.313
between work and free time and life.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:03.410
Work week is 37 1/2 hours

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:05.083
for everyone.

00:01:05.083 --> 00:01:05.916
- That's like average like, oh-

00:01:05.916 --> 00:01:07.600
- And then you have everybody is entitled

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.636
to at least seven weeks
of vacation a year.

00:01:10.636 --> 00:01:11.469
- Wow.

00:01:11.469 --> 00:01:12.687
- So that means that you can actually--

00:01:12.687 --> 00:01:13.520
(audience clapping)

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:14.353
- That's why you're so happy.

00:01:14.353 --> 00:01:15.186
- We're so happy, yeah.

00:01:15.186 --> 00:01:18.290
- It's like, did you take many vacations?

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:21.360
- Yeah, as a kid my mom was a single mom

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:25.240
and I have two sisters
and we didn't have much,

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.820
but she insisted that we'd
go away at least for a week,

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:30.220
somewhere outside of Denmark.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:33.390
We had this tiny little Fiat 600,

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:36.570
which is probably the
tiniest car you can imagine.

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.710
And then she would just grab
on our bicycles on the roof

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:42.650
and off we went to
normally it would be Sweden

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:47.140
or Scandinavia but we did one
trip once where we went to,

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.090
she wanted to go to Spain.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:49.910
She was like, this is it.

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:54.120
But she also had this rule
that she hated highways.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.740
She wanted us to see the
countries we went through

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:57.840
also cause the car--

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:58.690
- Why did she hate highways?

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:01.110
- I think also the car didn't
go faster than 60 miles

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:01.943
an hour so-

00:02:01.943 --> 00:02:04.050
- She was slowly in denial the whole time.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:07.970
- Every time when she hit 60
the car, was like (squeaking)

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:09.063
So there was that.

00:02:09.986 --> 00:02:12.910
And then I remember I
was surprised by how,

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:15.540
we were going through the
Pyrenees, the mountains.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.120
My mom is terrified of heights.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:18.980
So I had, and so are my sisters,

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:19.813
they were screaming the whole time,

00:02:19.813 --> 00:02:22.480
and so then suddenly the car broke down,

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:26.520
and I remember thinking I
was six or seven, I was like,

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:29.650
Oh my God, all the Spanish
men are so friendly.

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:32.840
'Cause say we had like every
time we broke down quite a bit

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.630
they would within two
minutes there would be a guy

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:36.680
they're helping.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.080
And now of course I look back,

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:40.760
oh yeah, you have a 32 year old,

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.407
beautiful Scandinavian
girl standing there going,

00:02:43.407 --> 00:02:44.491
"I don't Know what to do."

00:02:44.491 --> 00:02:45.690
- Those Spanish men weren't
helping all the ladies?

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:46.910
Just your mom?

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:49.970
- Yeah, we did have a couple of,

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:52.590
I still remember also
that we had some visitors

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:54.950
in Denmark from Spain (Kerry laughing).

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:56.173
- She's polite, they helped her out.

00:02:56.173 --> 00:02:57.557
- They were so friendly.

00:02:57.557 --> 00:02:59.140
(continued laughter)

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:01.850
- Wait, so now you have,
is it 21 and 18 year girls?

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:02.750
- I have, oh yes.

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:05.033
I have my beautiful daughters, 21--

00:03:05.033 --> 00:03:06.880
- So like that's kind of out of the house

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:08.320
kind of you're in to nest to try--

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:11.040
(applause)

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:12.930
Are you nervous about this scenario?

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:14.729
Like the guy situation, are you-

00:03:14.729 --> 00:03:15.562
- Oh.

00:03:15.562 --> 00:03:16.723
- 'Cause were you like
one of those guys that

00:03:16.723 --> 00:03:19.444
you should be afraid of
your daughters dating?

00:03:19.444 --> 00:03:20.281
(both laughing)

00:03:20.281 --> 00:03:23.083
- Yeah, exactly, exactly that.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:27.120
'Cause you have that thing
where sometimes you look back

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.620
and we like to romanticize
about those years

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:32.910
when we were younger or it were so great

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:35.010
high school, it was like,
there was nothing but joy

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:36.580
and then you start thinking and go,

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:38.290
well, actually it was pretty stressful

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:40.610
and you try to fit in
and you didn't know any,

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:42.010
I mean, all that.

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:44.280
So I think that was one of the things

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:47.720
when my kids and they're now 18 and 21

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:51.370
knowing that they had
to go through heartbreak

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:53.780
and all those things is devastating.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:55.970
But of course then the boys, right?

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:59.331
And the first-

00:03:59.331 --> 00:04:00.260
- Did you give them a hard time?

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:05.260
- No, the first time I was
introduced to a boyfriend.

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:08.790
I think I was more
freaked out than he was.

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:10.660
'Cause I remember I was like back then

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:13.830
when it was me I don't
know why I was terrified

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:17.050
of meeting parents but it is frightening.

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:19.360
- Do they date generally
pretty good guys though?

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:20.320
That's cool.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:21.600
- So far so good.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:22.880
I mean but then at the same time,

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:25.140
then you hear stories from their friends

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:28.800
and like there's, I mean,
there's a lot of frogs out there.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:30.680
- Yeah, but I'm just saying
like, I think that maybe

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:32.340
you've done a good job
of representing to them

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:34.340
like how a man should treat you.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:35.800
So maybe that's why they got good guys.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:36.713
- I hope so.

00:04:36.713 --> 00:04:37.546
(audience clapping)

00:04:37.546 --> 00:04:38.379
- Yeah.

00:04:38.379 --> 00:04:39.212
- I like that but yeah-

00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:40.045
- No, that's good.

00:04:40.045 --> 00:04:40.878
Just live in that fantasy world.

00:04:40.878 --> 00:04:42.410
- I like yeah, I stay in my fantasy,

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:43.290
yes, it's all good

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:44.240
- It's all great.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.727
Now let's all look at
his new Netflix movie

00:04:46.727 --> 00:04:48.600
"Against the Eyes," so tell us about it.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.510
I love movies like, is this like survival,

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:52.030
kind of like you're surviving a situation?

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:53.740
- It's based on a true story, you know?

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:54.573
- Oh, it is.

00:04:54.573 --> 00:04:57.560
- I'm so proud of this
movie, I really love it.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:00.110
And I've been looking to
do something in Greenland

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:01.920
for a long time, my wife's from Greenland,

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:04.643
so I've been coming there
for more than 20, 25 years.

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:09.320
And I found this book which
is just an insane, true story

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:13.760
of survival but also about companionship

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:16.860
and we have these, it's
about these two guys,

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:19.230
that get stuck in the North
Eastern part of Greenland.

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:21.000
They have to survive.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:22.010
But also what happens

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:23.080
when you're under that kind of pressure,

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:24.480
what happens with your mind?

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:25.380
- [Kelly] Mentally.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:28.450
- [Nikolaj] Mentally and he
just captured that beautifully

00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:31.100
and we wanted to explore
this in the movie and we did

00:05:32.293 --> 00:05:34.300
and then we wanted to shoot
everything on location

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:35.570
in the Arctic.

00:05:35.570 --> 00:05:37.320
So we shot in Iceland and in Greenland

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:41.310
and it was tough, it was extreme,

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:42.710
it was everything hoped for.

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:47.190
So, when you watch the movie
and you see a storm or-

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:48.023
- It's like real.

00:05:48.023 --> 00:05:50.000
- We were shooting in like,

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:51.140
I'd never done that before.

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:54.100
I mean, we were shooting on
these glaciers in Iceland.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:56.460
We all had to carry a whistle

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:59.800
in case we fell into a
crevasse so they could find us.

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:00.633
And the one day-

00:06:00.633 --> 00:06:02.100
- That's when I'm out (laughs).

00:06:03.173 --> 00:06:05.100
- But I mean, the crew, they
all knew what they were doing.

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:05.960
It was great.

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:06.990
I felt safe.

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:11.110
Well, there was that one day
where there was this storm

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:12.430
and they kept saying
it's gonna get better,

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:15.590
but it just got worse, there
were hurricane force winds

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:17.340
and suddenly we were all called in.

00:06:18.230 --> 00:06:20.410
Well, actually first we get this call that

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:23.060
this guy who was sitting
in a van had called in

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:26.910
that rocks were flying
through the windows of the van

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:30.070
but you couldn't make it
up and then we get a call,

00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:33.700
we have to gather at base
camp to do a roll call.

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:35.400
So everyone had to be evacuated

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:36.610
and then they had to call everyone,

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:37.610
and then incase you weren't there,

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:39.393
they had to set rescue teams out.

00:06:40.390 --> 00:06:42.743
So that was unusual.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:46.620
- You were really, you were method acting

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:47.820
you like wanted to get in there.

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:50.050
- But the thing is it's what
we wanted 'cause we, I mean,

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:51.025
- It's beautiful though.

00:06:51.025 --> 00:06:51.858
It probably looked beautiful

00:06:51.858 --> 00:06:56.830
- And like shooting in Greenland
on the actual sea ice is

00:06:58.840 --> 00:06:59.810
I mean you can't do that.

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:01.140
You can do everything in CGI

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:05.660
but you can't replicate
what it does to the actors

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:07.950
and to the faces and to the way you talk,

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:11.100
when you're actually
shooting in like 20 below

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:13.970
it's just, it does something special.

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:15.870
And then to, I mean, there
are so many things, like

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:18.880
obviously there's a lot
of, it's a true story,

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:21.000
this guy lived, there's a family.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:23.160
We wanted to do it right.

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:24.830
We had a screening for the family

00:07:24.830 --> 00:07:27.790
of my character two weeks ago.

00:07:27.790 --> 00:07:30.470
And I was really nervous
because of course we are like

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:32.360
all the dialogue is something we make up

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:34.530
and we change stuff, it's a movie.

00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:36.100
- Yeah.

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:39.709
- But they came out there
was like, they were so happy.

00:07:39.709 --> 00:07:41.270
- Oh, getting that approval's cool.

00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:42.103
- Yeah, and I was like,

00:07:42.103 --> 00:07:45.030
how I got this beautiful
letter from a grandchild

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:47.550
and she was like this, she
was so afraid of this movie

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:50.260
because it was part of her identity.

00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:53.220
That her great grandfather
did this and then like

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:55.130
and he was kind of this
secret in the family

00:07:55.130 --> 00:07:58.790
and she wanted to keep
it, like keep it special

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:01.610
but she was very very happy
and thrilled, so yeah.

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:03.670
- That's so cool.

00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:06.390
Wait, did you ever get
into like fantasy games

00:08:06.390 --> 00:08:08.750
like Dragons and Dungeons and Dragons,

00:08:08.750 --> 00:08:09.890
is that what it's called?

00:08:09.890 --> 00:08:11.650
I always have men on this
show that love that game.

00:08:11.650 --> 00:08:12.930
- Yeah, I love the idea.

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:16.833
No, I didn't, well, back
when I was in high school,

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:23.059
there was Dungeons and
Dragons was a big thing.

00:08:23.059 --> 00:08:23.892
It still is.

00:08:23.892 --> 00:08:24.725
- I didn't know it was a big thing,

00:08:24.725 --> 00:08:26.296
but I've had several
actors like men on here,

00:08:26.296 --> 00:08:27.630
it was like, "Yeah, I'm in a club."

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:29.170
I'm like what?

00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:32.530
- I never got into that
kind of role playing thing.

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:34.300
But I wanted to be part of this group

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:36.130
and then they said, "Yeah,
we're gonna play this

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:38.330
and then we gonna smoke a
bit of weed, we get high."

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:39.720
And I said, I don't smoke.

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:40.553
I can't do that.

00:08:40.553 --> 00:08:43.680
So they baked this cake for me

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:46.040
and I like cake.

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:47.270
- [Kelly] I love this story.

00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:50.010
- So we came in and they were doing stuff

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:51.200
and I just saw this cake

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:52.680
and I just assumed it was all for me.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:54.133
So I ate it all.

00:08:54.133 --> 00:08:55.106
- No.

00:08:55.106 --> 00:08:56.550
- And then they came in and
said, "Where's the cake?"

00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:58.210
And I said, well, it
was for me, wasn't it?

00:08:58.210 --> 00:08:59.920
And they got, "You ate all of,

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:01.070
that's a lot."

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:03.510
And I'm like, "No, it's
gonna be a fine, oh."

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:05.900
- It was weed cake, it had weed in it?

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:08.963
- It had like hash, hash or
whatever you call it, yeah.

00:09:08.963 --> 00:09:10.010
- Weed but hash, yeah.

00:09:10.010 --> 00:09:12.880
- Yeah, Yeah, I got a little weird.

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:16.000
- I Bet you got really into role playing.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:19.540
- I actually literally (chuckling)

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:21.300
I got into character
better than ever before.

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:22.557
I was literally

00:09:22.557 --> 00:09:23.390
(Kelly laughing)

00:09:23.390 --> 00:09:25.290
chasing my friends through the forest

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:26.123
and they were like, "Stop it"

00:09:26.123 --> 00:09:27.040
I'm like, "Ah"

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:30.540
and I don't know what the
character was, but it was intense.

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:31.780
Then we came back to the house

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:34.245
and I remember I was crying at one point

00:09:34.245 --> 00:09:35.120
(audience laughing)

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:36.027
and then I was laughing and-

00:09:36.027 --> 00:09:37.750
- The range of emotions.

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:39.720
- And then they said I
suddenly fell asleep.

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:42.050
It was literally like ah.

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:44.170
- Oh my God, you ate the whole cake.

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:45.440
- I ate the whole cake.

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:47.530
- Oh, and you didn't
know, that's not good.

00:09:47.530 --> 00:09:48.931
Nicolaj, (chuckling)

00:09:48.931 --> 00:09:50.181
that's so funnier though.

